    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Wildlife</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> The Daily Habits of Animals</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write instructions for a simple game or craft. Interactive: Write simple comments on a peer's short text.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write instructions for a wildlife guessing game (e.g., "Choose an animal card and describe its habitat."). Can provide steps for a nature craft related to animals (e.g., "Cut out shapes to make a sloth."). Can write supportive comments on a peer's description of wildlife (e.g., "I like your information about the jaguar.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different animals from Panama's wildlife (e.g., a sloth, a jaguar, a harpy eagle). Students are asked to quickly write one or two sentences describing one animal's habitat or a daily habit in their notebooks. For example: "The sloth lives in the forest. It moves very slowly." This activates prior knowledge and sets a writing-focused tone.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple set of instructions on the board for a basic action, like "How to draw a simple frog." (e.g., "1. Draw a big circle. 2. Add two small circles for eyes."). The teacher models clear, concise writing and uses imperative verbs. They then demonstrate how to write a comparative sentence: "The jaguar is faster than the sloth." and a sentence using 'there is/are': "There are many trees in the jungle."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the first step in these instructions?" (Pointing to the modeled instructions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you tell me another animal that lives in the forest like the jaguar?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reinforces pronunciation of "wildlife" (wild-life) by clapping syllables and asks students to repeat words with long/short vowel sounds like "snake" and "frog."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Panama's Wildlife".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a handout with two short texts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A set of simple instructions for a "Panama Wildlife Mask" craft (e.g., "How to Make a Sloth Mask: 1. Cut out a sloth face shape. 2. Draw big eyes. 3. Add gray fur details."). This text uses verbs like 'cut', 'draw', 'add' and nouns like 'shapes', 'sloth'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A short descriptive paragraph about the daily habits of a specific Panamanian animal (e.g., a tapir), incorporating "There is/are" and present simple verbs like "The tapir lives in the forest. It protects its young. There are many plants it eats."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Follow instructions in collaborative activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the craft instructions and match each step to corresponding pictures on the handout (information matching).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the animal description and answer True/False questions (e.g., "The tapir lives in the jungle. T/F").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher highlights the use of comparative adjectives in a sentence written on the board related to the tapir, e.g., "The tapir is heavier than a deer." and asks students to identify the comparative adjective.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing complete sentences using comparative and superlative adjectives, "There is/are," and present simple verbs to describe animals, habitats, and daily habits. Vocabulary includes: habitat, wildlife, jaguar, forest, predator, prey, species, adaptation, jungle, biodiversity, protect, live, grow, hunt, survive, adapt, migrate, observe, conserve, endangered, nocturnal, wild, diverse, camouflaged, resilient.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice:</b> Students complete cloze sentences related to Panama's wildlife.
                        <br><i>Examples:</i> "The sloth _______ (move) slowly. There _______ (be) many diverse _______ (species) in the Panamanian jungle. The jaguar is a _______ (predator). The harpy eagle is the _______ (strong) bird in Panama." (Teacher guides students to use present simple, 'there is/are', and superlatives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice:</b> Students are given jumbled sentence strips that form simple instructions for a basic animal-related craft or activity (e.g., "a frog drawing"). They must reorder the words to create coherent instructional sentences and then number the steps correctly.
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "cut / shapes / out / make / to / a / sloth" becomes "Cut out shapes to make a sloth."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students receive a prompt like "Compare two animals from Panama you learned about." Each student writes 3-4 sentences comparing the animals using comparative/superlative adjectives and describing their habitats/habits. (e.g., "The ocelot is smaller than the jaguar. It is more nocturnal. Both live in the forest."). After writing, they exchange notebooks and peer-check each other's sentences for correct grammar, vocabulary, and clarity. They briefly discuss pronunciation of key words like "nocturnal" or "camouflaged."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Panama's Wildlife".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Panama Wildlife Game or Craft Instructions"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work individually to write a set of instructions. They choose one of two options:</p>
                <ol style="list-style-type: upper-alpha; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option A: Wildlife Guessing Game Instructions:</b> Students write 4-5 steps for a simple guessing game about Panama's wildlife. The instructions should tell players how to play, including how to describe an animal using its habitat, daily habits, and at least two comparative or superlative adjectives.
                        <br><i>Example prompt:</i> "Write instructions for a game called 'Guess the Panama Animal'. How do players describe the animal? What information should they include? (e.g., "1. Choose an animal card. 2. Describe its habitat using 'There is/are'. 3. Talk about its daily habits using the present simple. 4. Use two comparative adjectives to compare it with another animal. 5. The other players guess the animal.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option B: Nature Craft Instructions:</b> Students write 4-5 steps for a nature craft related to a Panamanian animal (e.g., "How to Make a Paper Harpy Eagle"). The instructions should be clear and concise, using imperative verbs and relevant vocabulary.
                        <br><i>Example prompt:</i> "Write instructions on how to make a paper tapir. What materials do you need? What are the steps? (e.g., "1. Get brown paper. 2. Cut out a big body shape. 3. Add small ears...").</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency and accuracy in written expression, coherent organization of instructions, and appropriate use of target grammar (comparatives/superlatives, 'there is/are', present simple) and vocabulary (habitat, species, forest, hunt, adapt, nocturnal, diverse).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Peer Feedback and Self-Correction</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students exchange their written instructions from Stage 4 with a partner. Each student reads their peer's instructions and provides written feedback directly on the paper. The feedback should include:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One supportive comment (e.g., "I like your information about the jaguar!" or "Your steps are very clear.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One suggestion for improvement (e.g., "Maybe add one more adjective here?" or "Check the spelling of 'endangered'.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher monitors the peer feedback, ensuring comments are constructive and supportive. Students then take back their own instructions and make any necessary corrections based on the peer feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation/Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target grammar (comparatives/superlatives, 'there is/are', present simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of target vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to provide simple, supportive comments to a peer.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I can write instructions for now is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I still need help with (grammar/vocabulary/writing style)..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing instructions today? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can writing clear instructions help us protect Panama's biodiversity?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students write instructions and use target grammar? (e.g., "The explicit modeling of instruction writing was effective.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students struggling with descriptive writing or grammar usage? (e.g., "Provide sentence starters for comparative sentences next time.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better integrate the pronunciation and phonemic awareness goals into the writing stages?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on the descriptive and instructional writing by having students summarize information about Panama's wildlife and present a 21st-century skills project, possibly involving explaining their game or craft instructions to a wider audience, consolidating the vocabulary about habitats and adaptations. This will further enhance their ability to explain and integrate learned information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>